ly you can't socialize that  is a risk factor and the lack of  socialization can make me more likely to  commit suicide so it all makes absolute  sense and lastly another risk factor  financial stress financial insecurity  weighs heavily on US workers and it is  disproportionately affecting those  without university degrees often rural  white middle aged people in Middle  America so this is a real problem and  the the takeaway here is very clear this  is a suicide crisis that we're having  and we need to connect suicide to the  lack of access to mental health and the  stigma around mental health the problems  with wages and the problems with access  to health care even for those who are  cognizant of the reality that depression  and anxiety should be treated by mental  health professionals even if you can get  past the stigma the cost and access to  mental health services is also a factor  and this is all connected to life  expectancy so when we say hey listen  let's let the for-profit insurance  employer connected status quo remain  because of business reasons well you  could make that argument but that's not  a very ethical argument when you see how  it directly correlates to increased  death rates for many groups this  particular study focusing on middle-aged  white men and women very disturbing  stuff and we need to not forget this in  the context of discussions about  healthcare as we proceed after the break  I will interview cosmologists Lawrence  Krauss one of our favorite guests on the  show how can we think about what there  was before there was a universe he joins  me next
 

